s"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/602"} "Los Angeles Memorial Sports Arena, Los Angeles, California"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/25352"} "37 from 37 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Single elimination tournament."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The favorite was Korea’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/4363"} "Kim Dong-Gil"] " who had been runner-up to Cuba’s Carlos García at the 1982 World Championships. But because of the Soviet-led boycott, García could not compete, leaving the field seemingly open for Kim. In the quarter-finals he faced American " [:a {:href "/athletes/8745"} "Jerry Page"] " and lost by a 4-1 decision. This enraged the Korean boxing officials, who threatened to withdraw from the tournament, but later said they were doing it only to call attention to what was considered a pro-American bias among the judges. Page went on to win the gold medal, with unanimous decisions in the semi-finals and over Thailand’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/8340"} "Dhawee Umponmaha"] " in the final. Like all of the United States’s 1984 boxing gold medalists, Page turned professional, but had a desultory career, finishing in 1990 with 11 wins and 4 losses. Umponmaha never fought professionally as a boxer, but he did do so in Muay Thai kickboxing, using the name Khaopong Sitthichuchai."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/8745"} "Jerry Page"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/8340"} "Thawee Umponmaha"]] [:td {} [: